From: mscharged-port
Subject: Adapt the Korean SDK sources natively
Upstream-base: a353c8f2dfb34eb08fecd725965a46ea629428e3
Classification: native compiler/ABI compatibility

The Korean release builds some SDK files from its own copies (R4QK01/), with
a newer KPAD and DWC. They get the native adaptations their older
counterparts have: KPAD takes sqrt and the memory functions from the host C
library (0209); DWC copies the in-game name as u16 and sends it big-endian
(0729), reaches the NAS over plain HTTP, the profanity check included (0727),
and passes 0 for the integer of the NAT negotiation result (0721); its own
DWCi_Np_CPUCopyFast tests alignment on pointer-sized addresses.

Korean branches of shared files: the error reset in OSReset.c reads OS.c's
and OSExec.c's boot flags, which are the host's natively (a native game never
starts from NAND or a reboot), and RFL's own copy of copyChar2Additional_
measures the Mii name cells as RFL_WCHAR, as 0452 does for the older one.
DECOMP_FORCEACTIVE, which only the Korean branch of d_nhttp.c uses natively,
expands to nothing: it keeps strings in the Wii link, and its fake function's
(...) declaration is not C17 (Clang rejects it).

Korean-only units: NAND logging dates its lines with the host's
OSCalendarTime (mon, as in 0720), and the play-time expiry, which the host's
OSInit never arms (it does not call __OSInitPlayTime), stops natively instead
of rebooting through the Wii's thread queue and physical memory.

